14
00:00:37.771 --> 00:00:39.439
Thanks, Andrew. I'm excited to be back

15
00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:41.374
here and start like you mentioned from explaining

16
00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:43.143
what the tool is, how it works,

17
00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:45.211
all the way towards using it in parallel

18
00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:49.349
with many different tools including Git worktrees
and MCP servers.

19
00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:52.185
What we've seen over the last couple

20
00:00:52.185 --> 00:00:54.521
years is AI assisted coding has evolved rapidly.

21
00:00:54.521 --> 00:00:58.925
It started from maybe people asking LLMs
occasional coding questions.

22
00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:01.728
suggestions to then GitHub autocomplete to then,

23
00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:03.596
your various tools that became

24
00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:05.765
more and more autonomous.

25
00:01:05.965 --> 00:01:08.401
And Claude Code, when it was released,

26
00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:10.770
was definitely a step up in terms of the degree of agency

27
00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.807
or the amount of stuff that the coding assistant

28
00:01:13.807 --> 00:01:15.508
could do by itself. And so

29
00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:17.177
I think many people were surprised that

30
00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:19.813
you could set a task that Claude would work on for

31
00:01:20.246 --> 00:01:22.649
many minutes or sometimes even more than a few minutes.

32
00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:25.185
And now there are developers that are orchestrating

33
00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:28.421
not just a single Claude instance,
but even several of them.

34
00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:30.356
working in parallel on different parts

35
00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:32.625
