58
00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.485
You'll make use of many Claude Code features

59
00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:35.255
like planning, thinking modes,
creating parallel sessions,

60
00:02:35.355 --> 00:02:36.956
and managing Claude's memory.

61
00:02:37.357 --> 00:02:40.426
For the second example, we'll shift gears
and work with Jupyter notebooks

62
00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:42.228
to explore e-commerce data.

63
00:02:42.729 --> 00:02:45.999
We'll refactor notebooks using Claude Code,
remove redundant code,

64
00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:49.035
and create powerful dashboards with web applications.

65
00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:51.471
Finally, we'll move to create a visual mockup

66
00:02:51.938 --> 00:02:54.207
based in Figma and use Claude Code,

67
00:02:54.641 --> 00:02:56.976
the Figma MCP server and a different

68
00:02:56.976 --> 00:02:59.412
MCP server to import the design,

69
00:02:59.412 --> 00:03:03.416
iterate, test, and build agentically
a powerful front-end application.

70
00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:05.985
If you're not currently a Claude Code user,

71
00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:08.188
I think learning this set of

72
00:03:08.188 --> 00:03:10.723
ideas will give you a meaningful acceleration

73
00:03:10.723 --> 00:03:13.459
in the way that which you can engineer systems.

74
00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:15.828
And even if you are a current Claude Code user,

75
00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:18.565
I think having Ellie share these best practices

76
00:03:18.565 --> 00:03:21.334
with you in a comprehensive and systematic way,

77
00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:24.604
will, I hope, leave you with quite a few new things

78
00:03:24.604 --> 00:03:26.940
you try that will be useful. your work.

79
00:03:27.173 --> 00:03:29.509
I'd like to thank from DeepLearning.AI,
Hawraa Salami,

80
00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:32.045
who had contributed to this course.

81
00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:34.814
In the next video, Elie will share

82
00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:37.383
Claude Code's underlying architecture,

83
00:03:37.817 --> 00:03:41.120
and you might be surprised by
how simple the architecture is.

84
00:03:41.654 --> 00:03:44.357
Claude Code relies on just
a small number of tools to search

85
00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:46.526
for patterns within your code files,

86
00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:49.762
to list directories, look at files,
do regex.

87
00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:52.565
It does not rely on semantically embedding

88
00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:55.768
your code in the code base
or transforming it into

89
00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:58.371
searchable structure. And one of the things that

90
00:03:58.371 --> 00:03:59.906
I think has made Claude Code effective

91
00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:03.209
is how it agentically can read through your code

92
00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:08.114
to take notes in a file called code.md
to figure out autonomously

93
00:04:08.114 --> 00:04:10.416
what is going on your codebase to then

94
00:04:10.416 --> 00:04:12.685
drive decision-making on how to advance your code.

95
00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:15.154
That's right. And because of that, and not

96
00:04:15.154 --> 00:04:16.823
having a need to index the codebase,

97
00:04:16.823 --> 00:04:19.726
you can ensure the codebase stays local.

98
00:04:19.726 --> 00:04:22.095
We'll talk about some of the security ramifications with that.

99
00:04:22.095 --> 00:04:24.831
